      "19361": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "## Summary\n\n`tools::parse_Rd()` writes past a 256-byte stack buffer when it reports a syntax error at a token longer than about 240 bytes, which kills R on macOS (SIGTRAP) and is a stack-buffer-overflow under ASan. Fixes a reproducible crash on malformed Rd input.\n\n## Details\n\nIn `yyerror()` in `src/library/tools/src/gramRd.y`, `snprintf()` fills `ParseErrorMsg` with \"unexpected TEXT '<token>'\", and two unbounded `strcat()` calls then append \", expecting ...\". One line is enough: `\\link[{x}aaa...]{y}` with 300 `a`s, since an `[option]` holds a single item. R 4.6.1 and R-devel r90959 both crash.\n\nReproducible example: attached as `rd-yyerror-overflow-repro.R`; it parses that line in a child R process, which exits with status 133 without the patch and 0 with it.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `rd-yyerror-overflow.diff` (against r90959). It appends the clause with `snprintf()` into the space left, and copies the file name with `snprintf()` too, as `strncpy()` leaves it unterminated for paths of 255 bytes or more (found by reading the code). `gramRd.c` is regenerated with bison 3.8.2. A `tests/reg-fuzz.R` test is included; `make check` passes and ASan reports nothing.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/523, where CI results can be seen.\n\n## AI Disclosure\n\nThis patch was prepared with the assistance of Claude Code. The issue was discovered while verifying the patch for PR#19360.",
